## 1. Timeline and expected progression of the development work

The project covers the period July 2025 – April 2026 and is ongoing. The technical work falls into four phases — the first three are completed, the fourth is active and expected to continue for the next 6–12 months.

### Completed phases

**Phase A — RAG product in production and customer evaluation (≈ Q3 2025).**
- Original product based on a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline with embryonic tool-calling for table look-ups.
- Sold to ~7 customers and tested across multiple document types and use cases through structured weekly feedback sessions.
- Output evaluated against expected results in Excel review spreadsheets ("EPD analysis", "Normain Outputs Checklista").

**Phase B — Architecture decision and rebuild (Oct 2025 – early Feb 2026).**
- Fall 2025: accumulated evidence (failed extractions, customer feedback) showed RAG could not meet the precision, calculation, multi-step reasoning, and citation requirements of structured knowledge work.
- December 2025: a fully agentic architecture became technically feasible thanks to underlying LLM tool-use improvements together with our internal experiments on real customer datasets.
- Dec 2025 – Feb 2026: full rebuild from scratch — agentic 9-tool reasoning loop, proprietary virtual file system (content-addressable storage with symlink-based pointers, ripgrep-backed search), cell-by-cell generation, simplified UI.
- February 10, 2026: "Agentic mode" / Extractional AI launched. Launch milestones, owners and dates are in the shared launch plan in the Drive folder.

**Phase C — Post-launch hardening and customer-driven iteration (Feb 2026 – present).**
- Continuous merge cadence of roughly 50–220 production PRs per month, with a weekly demo, weekly priority sync and weekly product review.
- Active workstreams (verifiable from the git log of the last ~3 weeks): result-level chat, prompt improvement flow, review mode in the trust panel, narrative reasoning surfaced in the trust panel, image rendering in the file viewer, real-time data-source updates over Redis, vectorize-queue retry handling, citation row highlighting, confidence-level icons in the spreadsheet.
- Linear projects currently In Progress: Stability & Quality, Improved source references, Run comparison, Document framework 1.x.

### Expected progression (next 6–12 months)

- **Trust & verification UX:** Continue iterating on the trust panel — narrative reasoning, review mode integrated into the main edit flow (not a separate mode), prompt improvement loop, and approval workflow. Each iteration is validated against real customer datasets and qualitative feedback.
- **Source-reference accuracy:** Move from line-number-based citations (deterministic, already shipped) to PDF-page-accurate, character-span citations with split-screen review, comparable to what some vertical competitors offer. This is still experimental — there is no off-the-shelf approach that works generically across arbitrary PDF, Word and HTML inputs.
- **Speed and scale at audit volumes:** Investigation into batching, model routing, cache strategies, and concurrency limits to support the 100+ documents at once class of use case that audit and consulting customers ask for.
- **Web crawling / subdomain coverage:** Reliable enumeration, fetching, deduplication and indexing of arbitrary customer-supplied subdomain trees. Today the WebFetch and WebSearch tools work for single URLs and small fan-outs; large, gated, JS-rendered sites are unsolved.
- **Closed-loop evaluation harness:** Build an internal eval framework that can gate releases on output quality across a growing library of customer-validated golden cases. The Linear "Testing framework" project tracks this; it is itself partially a research problem because there is no canonical metric for agentic structured extraction.
- **Tool-set expansion:** New tools and refinements as customer use cases surface them — e.g. an exhaustive search tool with overlap-aware batching has just been added. The reasoning loop is expected to keep growing in capability rather than freezing.

There is no planned feature freeze. The expectation is that the agentic system will continue to evolve in close coupling with customer feedback for at least the next two years.

**Confidence: High** — phases, dates and active workstreams are directly verifiable from the Drive folder, the production git history, the Linear project board and recent customer-feedback artefacts. Forward-looking content (the "next 6–12 months" list) is grounded in the projects that are currently In Progress; exact ordering and durations may shift with customer pressure.

