## 2. How the technical uncertainties are evolving and which remain unresolved

Across this period the uncertainty profile has shifted markedly. Several large architectural risks have been retired; a different, narrower set of harder problems has come into focus.

### Resolved during the period

- Whether an agentic architecture would outperform RAG for structured knowledge work — resolved positively, validated by customer outputs, qualitative feedback and post-launch usage.
- Whether content-addressable storage with symlink-based pointers can serve as a usable virtual file system while a customer reorganises files mid-indexing — resolved positively. Performance was a major problem early in the rebuild and is now sufficient for current customer workloads. ContentAddressableStorage and Ripgrep-backed search are in production.
- Whether cell-by-cell generation can be made cost-tractable — resolved positively in principle (deployed to all customers), with ongoing concurrency and cost-management work.
- Whether the agentic loop is recoverable when individual reasoning steps fail — partially resolved: tools wrap LLM calls in retry-with-back-off and abort-signal handling, but failure-classification is still iterating.

### Reduced but not closed

- **Trust & verifiability UX.** Narrative reasoning, review mode and result-level chat have all reduced the trust gap reported in early 2026 customer feedback (Sam Methold, Olof Hällerman, Black Sun Global), but the most recent feedback still flags the raw tool-call trace as insufficient. Iteration is ongoing.
- **API-throughput stability under load.** Recent commits (e.g. "Fix vectorize queue failing on transient errors", the retries-with-back-off behaviour in the exhaustive-search tool) show this is a moving target rather than a one-shot fix.

### Still open and actively investigated

- **PDF page-level citation accuracy.** Line-number boundaries from the virtual file system are deterministic, but mapping them back to the source PDF page (with highlight) is heuristic and breaks on multi-column layouts, reflowed text and OCR'd scans. Customers consistently flag this as the dominant trust blocker (Black Sun Global, Pollen Street Capital, Sam Methold, Olof Hällerman). Linear project "Improved source references" is In Progress; no off-the-shelf approach solves it generically.
- **Reliable web crawling and subdomain coverage.** Customers expect Normain to find answers across any subdomain in a large website. Fetching, dedup, JS rendering, robots/auth handling and indexing of arbitrary subdomain structures is unsolved.
- **Speed and scale at audit volumes** (e.g. 100+ documents at once). Cell-level concurrency, LLM cost trade-offs and queueing are still bottlenecks. Customer feedback ("Ni tappar mig" — Olof; Sam Methold's audit executives prioritising scale and speed over feature complexity) confirms this is the next critical frontier.
- **Closed-loop evaluation / regression harness.** The repo has an evals package and a dedicated vitest eval config, but a quality bar that can gate releases across customer-validated golden cases does not yet exist. There is no canonical metric for agentic structured extraction; defining one is itself a research problem.
- **Validation / review mode integration.** Today it lives as a separate "mode"; customers (Sam Methold) explicitly want it folded into the main editing flow. The right interaction model is still being explored.
- **Tool-set scope.** Each new customer dataset surfaces tool gaps (e.g. mathematical operations once required an explicit CalculatorTool, table look-ups required TableRowSearchTool). Whether the current 9-tool set is the long-term steady state or whether more tools will be added is itself an open question.

**Confidence: High** — the open and resolved categories are directly traceable to the codebase, the customer-feedback PDF and the Leyton checklist. The split between "reduced" and "still open" involves judgement (e.g. trust UX is genuinely between the two) — those calls are made conservatively.

