## 3. Which technical challenges still take up most of the effort today and require investigation, testing and iterative experimental development

The work below is where the team is currently spending experimental effort — i.e. work where the right approach is not known in advance, prototypes are tested against real customer data, and approaches are frequently abandoned or reworked. It is contrasted with the work that does not qualify as R&D (routine UI polish, dependency upgrades, Azure alerts, etc.).

### Highest current effort — trust, verification and source-reference UX (~40%)

- Surfacing the agent's reasoning to non-technical users. Customer feedback consistently shows that listing raw tool calls is not enough; users want a narrative reasoning trace that says "from note 21 I found current provisions of £368M and noncurrent of £1,493M". Recent commits: "Add result-level chat, prompt improvement flow, and review mode to trust panel"; the redesigned canvas cell footer wired to verification and trust panel.
- Accurate source-reference citation: line-number-anchored citations exist; per-page PDF highlighting and split-screen review mode are still being prototyped (matched against the bar set by vertical competitors).
- Why this is R&D: there is no published solution that gives audit-grade citations for arbitrary input formats with agent-driven extraction; the team prototypes, tests on real customer files, and rolls back approaches that do not generalise.

### Agent loop quality and tool design (~25%)

- Prompt engineering for the reasoning loop in the three modes (single, repeat / loop, spreadsheet); anti-injection prompt scaffolding.
- Tool design and refinement. Examples: ExhaustiveSearchTool (overlap-aware batching with 2-chunk overlap, deduplication, batch-level retries with exponential back-off and abort propagation); SemanticSearchTool; GrepTool backed by ripgrep; CalculatorTool; AddSourceReferenceTool / AddTableReferenceTool.
- Failure-mode handling. Each new customer dataset exposes new failure shapes; the appropriate fallback or tool addition is not predictable from existing literature.

### Speed and scale at audit volumes (~15%)

- Concurrency tuning, batch-size tuning and cost-vs-latency trade-offs at the cell-by-cell level. The current parallel-promise wrapper has configurable concurrency, retries, exponential back-off, jitter and abort signals; tuning is still empirical, per use case.
- Queue and worker stability — vectorize queue retry behaviour, web-fetch error recovery, transient-error classification — all recent fixes.

### Web ingestion at scale (~10%)

- Subdomain enumeration, JS-rendered content, authenticated content, deduplication. WebFetchTool and WebSearchTool exist; reliable end-to-end ingestion of arbitrary subdomain trees is still experimental.

### Evaluation / regression infrastructure (~10%)

- Building golden-set evaluations, agreeing on a quality metric for agentic structured extraction, and wiring the eval harness into release gating. The repo has the scaffolding (evals package, vitest.eval.config.ts) but the metric design is still a research problem.

### What is NOT R&D today (for reference)

- Routine UI polish (column widths, scrollbar behaviour, button styling, breadcrumb tooltips).
- Dependency upgrades and CVE clean-up driven by Vanta.
- Standard infrastructure work (Azure monitor alerts, backup retention, Docker fixes).
- Compliance work (ISO 27001, SOC 2, Vanta posture).

These are excluded from R&D effort estimates above; they are straightforward implementation against established practice.

**Confidence: High for the thematic split, medium for the percentage allocations** — the categories and the work inside each category are directly verifiable from recent commits, Linear issues and the customer-feedback artefacts. The percentages are educated estimates based on commit volume, PR scope and Linear activity, not measured time tracking — they are intended as proportional indicators rather than precise figures.

